# Unity UI Toolkit

Applies whenever a task involves UI Toolkit: authoring or editing UXML and USS, a `UIDocument`, a screen controller, or presenting data to the player.

Read `unity-core.mdc` first. It carries the mandatory project standards, asset references, and compliance statuses. This file adds only UI-specific rules and does not restate them.

If no file in the task matches the globs but the task still involves UI, follow this file anyway.

## 1. UXML and USS authoring

This is the UI-specific half of `unity-core.mdc` principle 3: C# does not replace authored structure.

- All predefined UI structure is authored in UXML.
- All visual styling is authored in USS: layout, spacing, colours, fonts, states, and themes.
- Prefer USS classes over inline styles. A data-driven inline value, such as a progress bar width, is acceptable.
- Give every element that C# queries a `name` attribute in the UXML.
- Name USS classes by role, not by appearance.
- Keep gameplay rules out of UXML, USS, and controllers.
- C# is for querying elements, registering and handling events, updating data, managing UI state, communicating with game systems, and instantiating templates.

## 2. UI Builder workflow

Use Unity UI Builder as the preferred tool for creating and editing UXML and USS.

- Author or edit the screen in UI Builder, then wire behaviour in C#.
- Do not hand-write a visual tree in C# and port it later. Author it once, in the editor.
- Keep UXML and USS in `Assets/UI/`, one folder per screen.
- Screen assets are part of the deliverable. A controller without its UXML and USS is incomplete work. See `unity-workflow.mdc` section 3.

## 3. Static vs. dynamic UI construction

Decide this before writing code, and record the decision in the plan required by `unity-workflow.mdc` section 2.

C# must not construct static hierarchies. Prohibited:

- `new VisualElement()`, `new Label()`, `new Button()`, or other controls for predefined structure.
- Defining a whole screen in C#.
- Reproducing an existing UXML structure in runtime code.
- Extensive visual styling in C# where USS applies.

Runtime element creation is permitted only when the count or structure genuinely depends on runtime data:

- Inventory entries.
- Dynamic item lists.
- Dialogue choices.
- Procedural UI elements.
- Repeated instances of a reusable UXML template.

In every permitted case, author the visual structure as a UXML template and instantiate it. Do not hand-build the row and then populate it.

A screen being displayed at runtime does not justify constructing it programmatically. Runtime UI still originates from authored UXML.

- **Static:** fixed elements, labels, panels, fixed actions. Authored in UXML, referenced by name in C#.
- **Dynamic count:** rows, lists, choices, repeated widgets. The container is authored in UXML; each instance comes from a UXML template.
- **Dynamic structure:** rare. Still start from an authored UXML base and add only what runtime data requires.

If a judgement call could go either way, prefer authored. The cost of adding a UXML template is lower than migrating a screen built in code.

## 4. UI component architecture

| Layer | Owns |
| --- | --- |
| UXML | Static hierarchy, element definitions, reusable visual structures |
| USS | Layout, styling, themes, visual states |
| C# controller | Initialization, queries, event handling, updates, interaction |
| Game systems | Rules, data, state, persistence, business logic |

- One controller per screen or reusable component. A screen controller does not reach into another screen's tree.
- The controller does not own gameplay rules and does not mutate game state directly; it calls the owning system.
- The controller exposes no public styling surface. Appearance changes go in USS.
- The player-facing boundary follows `unity-core.mdc` section 5.

### Screen framing

Screens fill the viewport. This is a reading-heavy, button-driven game: the player is shown a described scene, offered options, and the chosen option leads to more text. The player never controls a character on screen, so the whole viewport is the interactive area.

- The root is `flex-grow: 1` with edge padding only. No centred card, no `max-width`, no side margins that waste the interactive area.
- The frame is a column that fills its parent.
- Header and footer are fixed and always visible. The content band between them is the only flexible region, so a tab with different content never moves the header or footer.
- Frequently-read values belong in the header, not behind a tab, because a tab change costs the player a navigation.
- Button targets are the primary interaction. Give them generous height and a visible hover and pressed state.

The rule and its reasoning are recorded in [DECISIONS/2026-11-07-full-screen-navigation-ui.md](../../../Projects/Docs/unity-second-life-system/Unity/unity-second-life-system/DECISIONS/2026-11-07-full-screen-navigation-ui.md).

### Tabbed screens

When a screen swaps between tabs or pages with `display: none`, the frame sizes itself to whichever page is in flow, so a page with less content shrinks the panel and moves the tab strip under the pointer.

A screen whose frame is height-independent does not have this problem: a full-bleed frame with a flexible content band keeps one height whatever a tab contains. Prefer that. When a frame's height still follows its content:

- Give the shared frame `min-height`, not `height`, so a taller page can still grow it.
- Derive the value from the measured height of the richest page, and name it as a custom property with a comment explaining why.
- Guard the invariant with a PlayMode test that switches tabs and compares the frame height. Layout values cannot be asserted from EditMode, which never builds a visual tree.

## 5. UI lifecycle and event handling

- `UIDocument` builds its visual tree in its own `OnEnable`. Query in `Start`, not `OnEnable`: component order among scripts on one `GameObject` is not guaranteed, so an `OnEnable` query can see an empty tree.
- Query once and cache the results. Do not re-query every frame.
- Do not resolve elements by child index or by walking `Children()`. Query by name.
- Register event callbacks in `OnEnable` and remove them in `OnDisable`, or register and unregister in the same method pair, so a disabled component stops responding.
- Unsubscribe from `clicked` on instantiated elements when you release them, so pooled or destroyed instances do not keep handlers alive.
- Null-check a required element after querying. Log an error naming the element and the object, then disable the component. Do not continue silently with a null element.

## 6. Reusable UI components

- A repeated visual structure becomes a UXML template asset, not a helper method that builds it in code.
- A composite widget with behaviour gets its own UXML asset and its own controller, with the `VisualTreeAsset` and `StyleSheet` serialized on that controller.
- Templates are instantiated from a serialized `VisualTreeAsset` reference, never loaded by path. See `unity-core.mdc` section 1.
- Shared styling lives in a shared USS file, not duplicated inline per screen.

## 7. UI-specific inspector configuration

- `UIDocument` gets its `VisualTreeAsset` and `PanelSettings` from the inspector, never from `AddComponent<UIDocument>()` or `ScriptableObject.CreateInstance<PanelSettings>()`.
- The controller serializes every known UI dependency: `VisualTreeAsset`, template `VisualTreeAsset`, `StyleSheet`, `PanelSettings`, fonts, sprites, and the data assets the screen presents.
- Prefer a UI prefab for a screen that is reused or opened per scene instance. Assign the `UIDocument`, `PanelSettings`, and controller fields on the prefab.
- Do not add a canvas or event system for UI Toolkit. Input arrives through the panel.

Serialization rules themselves are in `unity-core.mdc` section 4.

## 8. UI validation

Run these checks in addition to the standing conditions in `unity-core.mdc` section 6, and report them through `unity-workflow.mdc` section 6.

- Static UI is authored in UXML.
- Styling is authored in USS.
- No unnecessary programmatic UI construction exists.
- Known UI dependencies use serialized references.
- No `Resources` or path-based loading exists for a UI asset.
- Scene and prefab references are configured correctly.
- Dynamic elements use reusable UXML templates.
- Event callbacks are registered and removed correctly.
- Element queries happen after the tree exists and are cached.
- The implementation follows the existing project architecture.
- The console has been read for **warnings**, not only errors, after any UXML, USS, or TSS edit.

A UI change is not complete when its C# compiles. Open the scene or prefab and confirm the referenced assets resolve. Report which scene or prefab was opened.

### USS fails quietly, so the console is part of verification

Unity reports an unsupported USS property as a **warning** and drops the declaration. The file still imports, the build still succeeds, and the element renders as if the rule were never written. A mistyped property, a CSS property USS does not implement, or a `calc()` expression therefore looks identical to working CSS until someone measures the pixels.

This has already cost this project: `line-height` sat in three stylesheets, silently ignored, and was found only by accident during unrelated work.

- After editing a stylesheet, read the console filtered to warnings. A clean compile is not evidence the styles were applied.
- USS has no `calc()`. A sum must be a literal, and shared literals belong in `ValidationTheme.tss` rather than copied per screen.
- USS implements roughly a hundred properties. When a style appears to do nothing, check the property name against Unity's USS property reference before hunting for a layout bug.
- `StylesheetPropertyTests` (EditMode) guards the specific properties this project has reached for. It is a partial list, not a validator. Passing it does not mean a stylesheet is correct.
